steps.body:
A `std::string` is a sequence of `char`s, so you loop over it like a vector. Use range-for when you only need each character:

```cpp
for (char c : s) {
    if (c == 'e') {
        ++count;
    }
}
```

`char c` is a 1 byte copy of each character, which is cheap. Use an index loop when you need the position, or to look at a neighbor:

```cpp
for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.size(); ++i) {
    std::cout << i << ':' << s[i] << '\n';
}
```

To transform text, start from an empty string and append with `+=`, one character per iteration. The string keeps its characters in a buffer it owns and grows that buffer as needed, so appending in a loop is fast.

> Gotcha: compare a `char` with a char literal in single quotes. `c == 'a'` works, but `c == "a"` compares a char with a string literal and does not compile.

example.code:
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    const std::string text = "programming in c plus plus";

    int vowels = 0;
    for (char c : text) {
        if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u') {
            ++vowels;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "Vowels: " << vowels << '\n';

    std::string underscored;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < text.size(); ++i) {
        if (text[i] == ' ') {
            underscored += '_';
        } else {
            underscored += text[i];
        }
    }
    std::cout << underscored << '\n';
    return 0;
}

