steps.body:
**Splitting.** Scan the characters and build the current word. At a space, push the word into a `std::vector<std::string>` and start over with `current.clear()`, which empties the string. After the loop, the last word is still in `current` because no space followed it, so push it too:

```cpp
for (char c : sentence) {
    if (c == ' ') {
        if (!current.empty()) {
            words.push_back(current);
            current.clear();
        }
    } else {
        current += c;
    }
}
if (!current.empty()) {
    words.push_back(current);
}
```

`push_back` copies `current` into the vector, so clearing `current` afterward does not touch the stored word. The `empty()` checks skip doubled spaces.

**Reversing.** Loop from the last index down to 0 and append. Use an `int` index: a `std::size_t` is never below 0, so `i >= 0` stays true and the loop reads past the start of the string.

**Palindromes.** Put `i` at the front and `j` at the back, compare, and move them toward each other until they meet. Stop at the first mismatch. Keep `j` an `int` so it cannot wrap around on an empty string.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    const std::string sentence = "the quick brown fox";
    std::vector<std::string> words;
    std::string current;
    for (char c : sentence) {
        if (c == ' ') {
            if (!current.empty()) {
                words.push_back(current);
                current.clear();
            }
        } else {
            current += c;
        }
    }
    if (!current.empty()) {
        words.push_back(current);
    }
    for (const auto& word : words) {
        std::cout << word << ' ' << word.size() << '\n';
    }

    const std::string original = "stressed";
    std::string reversed;
    for (int i = static_cast<int>(original.size()) - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
        reversed += original[i];
    }
    std::cout << reversed << '\n';

    const std::vector<std::string> candidates = {"level", "hello"};
    for (const auto& word : candidates) {
        int i = 0;
        int j = static_cast<int>(word.size()) - 1;
        bool isPalindrome = true;
        while (i < j) {
            if (word[i] != word[j]) {
                isPalindrome = false;
                break;
            }
            ++i;
            --j;
        }
        std::cout << word << (isPalindrome ? " is a palindrome" : " is not a palindrome") << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

